@Service
public class IdempotencyService {
    private final IdempotencyRepository repo;
    private final TransactionTemplate txTemplate;
    private final ObjectMapper objectMapper;
    private final Clock clock;
    private final Map<String, Object> locks = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public ServiceResult execute(String endpoint, String requestId, Object businessPayload, Supplier<ServiceResult> business) {
        String fingerprint = fingerprint(requestId, businessPayload);
        Object lock = locks.computeIfAbsent(requestId, k -> new Object());
        synchronized (lock) {
            try {
                return txTemplate.execute(status -> {
                    Optional<IdemRecord> existing = repo.findByRequestId(requestId);
                    if (existing.isPresent()) {
                        IdemRecord rec = existing.get();
                        if (!rec.fingerprint().equals(fingerprint)) {
                            throw new IdempotencyConflictException(...);
                        }
                        return new ServiceResult(rec.responseStatus(), readTree(rec.responseBody()));
                    }
                    ServiceResult result = business.get();
                    repo.insert(requestId, fingerprint, endpoint, result.status(), writeJson(result.body()), LocalDateTime.now(clock));
                    return result;
                });
            } finally {
                locks.remove(requestId, lock);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Wait — removing lock in finally could allow a third thread to create a new lock while second waits on old lock — classic issue: thread A holds lock L, thread B waits on L, A removes L from map and releases; thread C computes new lock L2 and enters, B also enters → concurrent. But both B and C would then find the existing record in DB (committed by A) → both replay. Since business already committed, no double execution. The only risk: A hasn't committed when it releases lock? No — txTemplate.execute commits before returning, and finally runs after. So removal happens post-commit. B and C both see committed record. Safe.

But if business throws (e.g., 404), no record inserted, tx rolled back, lock removed. B retries business → same exception. Fine — errors are not idempotency-replayed (acceptable; requirement says same key same params return first result — for successful writes; error semantics: re-executing yields same error deterministically). Hmm, "同键同参返回首次结果" — strictly should also replay errors? Common practice: only successful responses are cached. I'll cache successful business results (2xx). Business exceptions propagate and aren't stored. Document this.

Fingerprint: sha256 hex of requestId + "\n" + objectMapper.writeValueAsString(payload). Payload DTO includes requestId too — fine.

Response body stored as JSON string; replay: objectMapper.readTree(body) → JsonNode returned as body. Controller returns ResponseEntity.status(result.status()).body(result.body()).

